Hi Bob,

To clarify about the constraint, of the constraints on my initial parameter g, I wished to keep the upper bound, so g<1. I then reparameterised my model in terms of g_3, which has a lower bound at 0 instead. I can see how this might not have been clear from what I wrote, hopefully this clears up any confusion.

Sorry about the plots being unclear. Each coloured line is the traceplot for a run of HMC. The x-axis is iteration number. You're right that the y axis should be labelled g_3 rather than chain, I'm plotting the current value of the parameter at each iteration.

Michael

From ud8614c9819918597 at groups.google.com  Tue Dec  1 14:40:54 2015
From: ud8614c9819918597 at groups.google.com (Olive Buhule)
Date: Tue, 01 Dec 2015 14:40:54 +0000
Subject: Cholesky decomposition: cholesky_factor_corr
Message-ID: <6BT0osWTcFA.79GeFwd6CwAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/6BT0osWTcFA/m/79GeFwd6CwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I have a similar question on estimating the correlation using lkj_corr_cholesky(eta).  For instance,  I have 4*4 correlation  matrix

                            1.0  0.6  0.6  0.6
      Omega_b=    0.6  1.0  0.6  0.1      and  standard deviations, sigma_b=c(0.134,0.182,0.179,0.255) for 4 random effects.
                           0.6  0.6  1.0  0.6
                           0.6  0.1  0.6  1.0

 I am using the relation (b <- mean + (diag_pre_multiply(sigma_b, L_Omega_b)*z)'

where

sigma_b ~ cauchy(0, 2.5);
to_vector(z)~normal(0,1);
L_Omega_b ~ lkj_corr_cholesky(2);

Omega_b <-multiply_lower_tri_self_transpose(L_Omega_b);

I am getting the following results:

Omega_b[1,1]      1.000
Omega_b[1,2]      0.268
Omega_b[1,3]      0.079
Omega_b[1,4]     -0.012
Omega_b[2,1]      0.268
Omega_b[2,2]      1.000
Omega_b[2,3]     -0.042
Omega_b[2,4]     -0.177
Omega_b[3,1]      0.079
Omega_b[3,2]     -0.042
Omega_b[3,3]      1.000
Omega_b[3,4]     -0.050
Omega_b[4,1]     -0.012
Omega_b[4,2]     -0.177
Omega_b[4,3]     -0.050
Omega_b[4,4]      1.000

What is the appropriate value of eta to be used?  I have used 1 and 2 and none of them is estimating the correlations correctly. I am able to estimate the standard deviations well with half Cauchy prior.  Is there something I am doing wrong?

Thanks,

Olive

