I see --- I thought they were parameters in the first discussion
and didn't look at the model more closely.

> if for K = 1 c is 2 and a is 2, you are saying that they are anyway unconstrained, so they can take values different from the ones I fix them to be (2,2)? I didn't realize a variable defined in data block can be unconstrained in the model. I thought (i.e., read) the constrains in data block were just needed for a sanity check.

That's right --- in the data and transformed data block, the constraints
only provide error checking.  Same for transformed parameters and generated
quantities.

>
> I'm not sure if you realized that Stan's normal distribution
> is parameterized with scale (or standard deviation), not with
> precision (1 / std_dev^2).  So the above means you're constraining
> beta[k] to be very very close to c[k] + a[k] * sigma[m].

Was this your intention?  If so, it's going to be hard on Stan's
adaptation.

What's on line 32 of your program?

I don't understand what your model's doing, but you can track
down where variables get out of hand using print statements or
conditional print statements.

- Bob

