Bob,

Thanks for your help.

On Saturday, December 26, 2015 at 1:57:36 PM UTC-5, Bob Carpenter wrote:
>
> > On Dec 26, 2015, at 10:50 AM, Robert Corty <rco...@gmail.com> wrote:
> >
> >
> > Michael,
> >
> > Thanks for your response.  It seems that in my attempt to make a toy example of my problem, I introduced a load of simple errors that obscured the problem.
>
> Specifically, you can't assign a real value to a vector.

Got it.  I believe this error is corrected in the code I included in my most recent post.

>
> >  I can deal with all those errors, and I've included below my draft of the model I really want to build, along with an R program to simulate data for it, which I think is clean from those types of errors.
> >
> > The key piece of the discussion is your recommendation to add this line to the model block:
> >
> > w ~ normal(a + b, sigma);
> >
> > But I think that conveys a pretty different idea than what I'm going for.  I don't believe that my observation is the sum of a, b, and noise.  I actually believe that my observation is an essentially noiseless function of a and b.
>
> I'm not sure what "essentially noiseless".  Either y = a + b, or it equals a + b + noise.

When I wrote "essentially noiseless", I meant "noiseless".  Deterministic.

>
> If it's the latter, then you want to fit what noise is there in the data.
>
> The problem is that if just a and b are scalars, then your model isn't
> identified.  See the problematic posteriors chapter of the manual for
> just this example.

a and b (from the toy example) as well as a and d (from the "real" model I want to fit) should all be vectors.  I believe I've corrected this in my most recent post.

>
> And you can't fit a prior based on one obseration.  Are a and b
> supposed to be vectors?
>
> >  Like a mathematical function where the input precisely specifies the output.  I believe my observation is the sum of a and b.  Maybe I should use the modeling approach you suggest and place a strong prior on sigma that keeps it very close to zero?  It seems that any non-zero-ness of sigma, however, would hurt my estimates of the parameters of the distributions from which a and b come.
>
> The big problem is that if a and b really are scalars, then every
> value has to be the same.
>
> >
> >
> >
> >
> >
> >
> >
> >
> >
> > The idea here is that I arrive at some time during a process (my co-worker taking a shower in a shared locker room), and I want to infer the mean and standard deviation of his shower durations.  I assume that I arrive at a time uniformly distributed between the start and end of the shower.
>
> Sounds like (part of) a homework problem.  What's the rest?
> That problem's not well defined without more constraint.

It is not related to homework in any way.  I really jog to work and wait for this guy to finish his shower.  It feels well-defined, but maybe you're right.  What additional information can you imagine might be necessary?  I'm willing to assume his shower durations are normally-distributed (truncated to be positive) and that my arrival is uniform within the shower duration.  The one-level-simpler formulation is:  If I were to assume that each shower of his has equal duration, I figure I could estimate the duration by doubling my average wait time (I think).

>
> - Bob
>
> >
> > library(rstan)
> > options(mc.cores = parallel::detectCores())
> > library(truncnorm)
> >
> > N <- 100
> > mu <- 35
> > sigma <- 5
> >
> > durations <- rtruncnorm(n = N, mean = mu, sd = sigma, a = 0)
> > arrivals <- runif(n = N, min = 0, max = durations)
> > waits <- durations - arrivals
> >
> > fit1 <- stan(file = 'z_shower_waiting.stan',
> >              data = list(N = N, w = waits))
> >
> > data {
> >   int<lower=1> N;   // number of observations
> >   vector[N] w;      // observed wait time
> > }
> >
> >
> > parameters {
> >   real<lower = 0> mu;
> >   real<lower = 0> sigma;
> >   vector<lower = 0.1>[N] d; // duration of shower
> >   vector[N] a;              // arrival time of waiter (me)
> > }
> >
> >
> > model {
> >   d ~ normal(mu, sigma);
> >   a ~ uniform(0, d);
> >   w <- d - a;
> > }
> >
> >
> >
> > I am met with the following error:
> >
> > SYNTAX ERROR, MESSAGE(S) FROM PARSER:
> >
> > attempt to assign variable in wrong block. left-hand-side variable origin=data
> >
> > ERROR at line 16
> >
> >  14:      d ~ normal(mu, sigma);
> >  15:      a ~ uniform(0, d);
> >  16:      w <- d - a;
> >               ^
> >  17:    }
> >
> >
> > PARSER EXPECTED: <expression assignable to left-hand side>
> > Error in stanc(file = file, model_code = model_code, model_name = model_name,  :
> >   failed to parse Stan model 'z_shower_waiting' due to the above error.
> >
> >
> >
> >
> >
> >
> >
> >
> >
> >
> >
> >
> > On Friday, December 25, 2015 at 9:25:16 PM UTC-5, Michael Betancourt wrote:
> > >
> > > data {
> > >   int<lower=1> N;   // number of observations
> > > }
> > >
> > > parameters {
> > >     real mu_a;
> > >     real mu_b;
> > >     real<lower = 0> sigma;
> > >     real a;
> > >     real b;
> > >  }
> > >
> > > model {
> > >     mu_a ~ normal(0, 10);
> > >     sigma_a ~ gamma(1, 1);
> > >     a ~ normal(mu_a, sigma_a);
> > >
> > >     alpha_b ~ normal(2, 5);
> > >     beta_b ~ normal(2, 5);
> > >     b ~ gamma(alpha_b, beta_b);
> > > }
> > >
> > > generated quantities {
> > >     vector[N] w;      // observed quantity
> > >     w <- a + b;
> > > }
> >
> > > And the error I get is:
> > >
> > > PARSER EXPECTED: <expression assignable to left-hand side>
> > > which points to the line with "w <- a + b;”
> >
> > Something is suppressing most of the parser error message.  That’s
> > just the last bit.
> >
> > The immediate problem is that you’re trying to assign a scalar (a + b)
> > to a vector (w), which is not possible.  You have to broadcast the
> > scalar values to each component of the vector, for example by
> >
> > w <- rep_vector(a + b, N);
> >
> > The next problem is that a measurement model needs to go in the model
> > block, not generated quantities.  I have no idea what model you
> > really want to build, but I imagine it would be something like
> >
> > model {
> >     mu_a ~ normal(0, 10);
> >     sigma_a ~ gamma(1, 1);
> >     a ~ normal(mu_a, sigma_a);
> >
> >     alpha_b ~ normal(2, 5);
> >     beta_b ~ normal(2, 5);
> >     b ~ gamma(alpha_b, beta_b);
> >
> >     w ~ normal(a + b, sigma);
> > }
> >
> > Then there’s the problem that alpha_b and beta_b and sigma_a and
> > sigma_b need to be defined somewhere.  Also b needs to be declared
> > as positive or you’ll run into problems.
> >
> > Long story short, your model needs lots of work.  I recommend playing
> > with some of the simpler examples in the manual first.
