> On Dec 31, 2015, at 12:22 PM, Cole Monnahan <mon...@uw.edu> wrote:
>
> Andrew: Yes, I would assume so. Caveat the following with the fact I'm a student just learning all this myself... What makes ADMB so powerful is AD, a template language, and compiled C++ -- all of which Stan also has. ADMB has some additional nice features since it was designed for optimization. I could see the fishery and ecology communities moving to Stan over time but there's a ton of legacy ADMB code so that might be a slow transition.

We're already seeing people in that community use Stan.
And in the closely related population ecology fields.  Always
amazing to me how intellectual communities organize.

> For instance, many fishery stock assessments done by the government are using a 20,000 line ADMB model (stock synthesis; Methot and Wetzel, 2011) which realistically no one is going to reproduce in Stan anytime soon.

How do you debug a 20K line ADMB model?  We still haven't figured
that out for Stan.  For something that big, we'd write the functions
in C++ anyway, and we know how to test that.

> Our field has relied on ADMB since the 1990s because it was the best (only?)  option for robust non-linear optimization at the time, so it is really exciting to see the development of Stan and what it has to offer moving forward.

It's the combination of autodiff (or symbolic) gradients and
a simple language that's the key.  I also don't know of anything
other than ADMB that did that.  There were plenty of C++ and
autodiff packages, but nothing as far as I know putting them
together.  Having a big community of users, like the fisheries and
wildlife people for ADMB is also a huge benefit for everybody.

- Bob

From ua8b6459b77c849fe at groups.google.com  Thu Dec 31 17:52:42 2015
